    {'document_id': 1, 'path': 'documents/DOC-FS-001.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 1, 'title': 'Wrenford Global Quality Equity Fund — Fund Factsheet', 'document_type': 'fund_factsheet', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 1, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-001'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Global Quality Equity Fund — Fund Factsheet\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-FS-001  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## Investment objective and process\nThe fund seeks long-term capital growth from established businesses with repeatable earnings and manageable debt. The manager combines company research with valuation discipline and normally holds investments for several years. Portfolio construction favours businesses that can finance investment from operating cash flow. The portfolio is deliberately concentrated in 25 positions; it is not a representation of the entire global equity market.\n\nThe strategy has no blanket tobacco, weapons or fossil-fuel exclusion. A favourable fund-level ESG assessment therefore does not demonstrate compliance with an individual client's ethical restrictions. Redleaf Tobacco, Ironvale Defence and Asterion Energy are permitted by the fund's own strategy but may be prohibited by a client mandate.\n\n## Principal risks and monitoring\nThe largest company positions and the combined technology allocation can dominate short-term results. Unhedged overseas holdings create currency risk for a sterling investor. Company shares may fall sharply during earnings disappointments, and diversified geography does not remove that risk. The manager reviews debt refinancing schedules and customer concentration alongside valuation. The risk rating describes this fictional fund's risk band and must not be treated as a portfolio suitability decision.\n\n## Stewardship update\nDuring the quarter the team challenged LumenArc Systems on electricity demand from new computing facilities and requested more detail from Asterion Energy on capital expenditure. Engagement is recorded as an action, rather than evidence that either investment meets a client's exclusions.\n\n## Fund characteristics and performance\n\n| Metric | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fund ID | FND-001 |\n| Manager | Mara Ellison |\n| Share class | GBP accumulation |\n| Investment strategy | Global large-cap equities |\n| Benchmark | Wrenford Synthetic World Equity Index |\n| Q3 2026 net return | 3.2% |\n| Q3 benchmark return | 4.0% |\n| Relative return, arithmetic | -0.8 percentage points |\n| Trailing 12-month net return | 9.4% |\n| Management fee, annual | 0.75% |\n| Other ongoing costs, annual | 0.15% |\n| Ongoing charges, annual | 0.90% |\n| Estimated transaction costs, annual | 0.08% |\n| Performance fee | None |\n| Internal risk rating | 5 / 7 |\n| Internal ESG score | 72 / 100 |\n| Holdings coverage | 100% of NAV; 25 securities; no residual cash, derivatives or fund-of-funds positions |\n\nReturns are measured in sterling with income reinvested, net of ongoing charges. Transaction costs are reflected in the fund value. Adviser and platform fees are excluded. All return periods end on 30 September 2026. These simulated values do not describe actual investments.\n\n## Complete holdings at 30 September 2026\n\n| Issuer ID | Company / security | Sector | Issuer domicile | Fund weight | Issuer ESG | Restricted activity tags |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ISS-001 | LumenArc Systems | Technology | United States | 8.00% | 88 | None |\n| ISS-002 | Bellmere Devices | Technology | United States | 7.00% | 78 | None |\n| ISS-003 | QuantaBridge Semiconductors | Technology | Taiwan | 6.00% | 75 | None |\n| ISS-004 | Northhaven Bank | Financials | United Kingdom | 6.00% | 73 | None |\n| ISS-005 | Alderwell Health | Healthcare | United Kingdom | 5.00% | 89 | None |\n| ISS-006 | CobaltGrid Utilities | Utilities | Germany | 5.00% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-007 | Cedarcrest Retail | Consumer Discretionary | United States | 5.00% | 70 | None |\n| ISS-008 | Pelagos Logistics | Industrials | Netherlands | 4.50% | 71 | None |\n| ISS-009 | Meridian Cloudworks | Technology | United States | 4.50% | 84 | None |\n| ISS-010 | Brackenridge Foods | Consumer Staples | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 77 | None |\n| ISS-011 | Asterion Energy | Energy | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 42 | fossil_fuels |\n| ISS-012 | Redleaf Tobacco | Tobacco | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 22 | tobacco |\n| ISS-013 | Ironvale Defence | Weapons | United States | 3.50% | 35 | weapons |\n| ISS-014 | Seabrook Telecom | Telecommunications | United Kingdom | 3.50% | 74 | None |\n| ISS-015 | Glasswater Insurance | Financials | Switzerland | 3.50% | 81 | None |\n| ISS-016 | Halcyon Medical | Healthcare | United States | 3.00% | 85 | None |\n| ISS-017 | Norwyn Industrials | Industrials | Germany | 3.00% | 68 | None |\n| ISS-018 | Stoneford Materials | Materials | Australia | 3.00% | 56 | None |\n| ISS-019 | Everfield Renewables | Utilities | Denmark | 3.00% | 92 | None |\n| ISS-020 | Greenhaven Water | Utilities | United Kingdom | 2.50% | 90 | None |\n| ISS-021 | Oakspire Housing | Real Estate | United Kingdom | 2.50% | 80 | None |\n| ISS-022 | Silvermere Rail | Industrials | United Kingdom | 2.50% | 83 | None |\n| ISS-023 | Auren Mobility | Consumer Discretionary | Germany | 2.50% | 81 | None |\n| ISS-024 | Clearstream Payments | Financials | United States | 2.50% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-025 | Solenne Agriculture | Consumer Staples | France | 2.00% | 86 | None |\n\n**Total fund weight: 100.00%.** Issuer domicile is a classification field; it is not a breakdown of revenue exposure. ESG scores in this table assess issuers, while the headline fund ESG score is a separate approved fund assessment.\n\n## Sector allocation\n\n| Sector | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| Technology | 25.50% |\n| Financials | 12.00% |\n| Utilities | 10.50% |\n| Industrials | 10.00% |\n| Healthcare | 8.00% |\n| Consumer Discretionary | 7.50% |\n| Consumer Staples | 6.00% |\n| Energy | 4.00% |\n| Tobacco | 4.00% |\n| Weapons | 3.50% |\n| Telecommunications | 3.50% |\n| Materials | 3.00% |\n| Real Estate | 2.50% |\n\n## Restricted-activity exposure\n\n| Activity | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| tobacco | 4.00% |\n| weapons | 3.50% |\n| fossil_fuels | 4.00% |\n\nThe activity calculation includes every position in a tagged issuer, including corporate bonds. A blank activity list means no restricted activity has been identified in the verified synthetic register; missing classifications in other datasets must trigger review. Refer to DOC-POL-001 for assessment rules and DOC-POL-003 for fee treatment.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-001'},
    {'document_id': 2, 'path': 'documents/DOC-COM-001.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 2, 'title': 'Wrenford Global Quality Equity Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary', 'document_type': 'manager_commentary', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 1, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-001'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Global Quality Equity Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-COM-001  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n**Period:** 1 July–30 September 2026  \n**Author:** Mara Ellison, Fund Manager\n\n## Global quality lagged a stronger market\n\nThe fund returned 3.2% in Q3 compared with 4.0% for its fictional benchmark, leaving an arithmetic shortfall of 0.8 percentage points. This is a comparison of total returns rather than an audited security-level attribution. The manager's assessment is that cautious positioning in highly valued technology shares limited participation in a strong part of the market.\n\nLumenArc Systems and Meridian Cloudworks benefited from sustained demand for computing services. Northhaven Bank supported returns following resilient lending income. These gains were partially offset by weaker pricing assumptions at Asterion Energy and slower order conversion at Norwyn Industrials. The manager also describes the sterling translation of overseas earnings as a modest headwind. No separate numerical currency attribution is available in this document.\n\nThe team reduced some exposure to Bellmere Devices during the quarter after reviewing its valuation and added to Alderwell Health when projected cash generation improved. These are qualitative descriptions of trading during the period; the full end-quarter holdings appear in the September factsheet. They should not be used to reconstruct transaction amounts or earlier weights.\n\nThe portfolio continues to include Redleaf Tobacco and Ironvale Defence. Their presence is consistent with this fund's own strategy but conflicts with zero-tolerance restrictions in the Haven mandate. The fund manager cannot waive a client restriction by describing these investments as defensive.\n\nThe next review will focus on earnings revisions, refinancing costs and the balance between technology concentration and valuation. This outlook sets monitoring priorities rather than forecasting returns. For a client explanation, the key message is that the fund gained value but lagged its comparator, and its permitted holdings still need to be checked against the client's rules.\n\n## Source and interpretation\n\nThe matching period-end factsheet is DOC-FS-001. Commentary explains the manager's view; numeric holdings and fees come from the current structured snapshot. No quantitative security attribution, future return forecast or investment recommendation is supplied.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-001'},
    {'document_id': 3, 'path': 'documents/DOC-FS-002.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 3, 'title': 'Wrenford Sustainable Leaders Fund — Fund Factsheet', 'document_type': 'fund_factsheet', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 2, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-002'}, 'content': '# Wrenford Sustainable Leaders Fund — Fund Factsheet\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-FS-002  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## Investment objective and process\nThe fund seeks capital growth from companies supporting resource efficiency, essential services and lower environmental impact. Research focuses on the economic durability of the business as well as the credibility of its transition plans. Holdings span electricity networks, water infrastructure, medical diagnostics, recycling and selected digital services. A positive environmental narrative alone does not qualify a company for investment.\n\nThis strategy excludes issuers carrying the tobacco, weapons or fossil_fuels activity tags in the approved issuer register. Those exclusions apply to the issuer, not only its sector label. The investment team checks the register before purchases and following classification changes. The current register identifies no excluded activity in the 25 positions shown below.\n\n## Principal risks and monitoring\nSustainable infrastructure businesses can be sensitive to changes in financing costs, subsidy arrangements and project delays. Several holdings share customers or supply chains, so owning different companies does not guarantee independent risks. The fund also holds technology companies and remains exposed to equity-market volatility. Its strong internal ESG score is a research assessment; it is not an externally certified sustainability label or a guarantee of positive environmental outcomes.\n\n## Stewardship update\nThe team requested evidence of battery recycling controls from Suncrest Storage and water leakage reduction from Greenhaven Water. Outstanding engagement actions remain open until supporting evidence is received. A fund can retain an investment while seeking improvements, provided the issuer has not breached an exclusion.\n\n## Fund characteristics and performance\n\n| Metric | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fund ID | FND-002 |\n| Manager | Theo Calder |\n| Share class | GBP accumulation |\n| Investment strategy | Global sustainable equities |\n| Benchmark | Wrenford Synthetic Sustainable Equity Index |\n| Q3 2026 net return | 4.6% |\n| Q3 benchmark return | 4.1% |\n| Relative return, arithmetic | +0.5 percentage points |\n| Trailing 12-month net return | 11.2% |\n| Management fee, annual | 0.85% |\n| Other ongoing costs, annual | 0.15% |\n| Ongoing charges, annual | 1.00% |\n| Estimated transaction costs, annual | 0.08% |\n| Performance fee | None |\n| Internal risk rating | 4 / 7 |\n| Internal ESG score | 86 / 100 |\n| Holdings coverage | 100% of NAV; 25 securities; no residual cash, derivatives or fund-of-funds positions |\n\nReturns are measured in sterling with income reinvested, net of ongoing charges. Transaction costs are reflected in the fund value. Adviser and platform fees are excluded. All return periods end on 30 September 2026. These simulated values do not describe actual investments.\n\n## Complete holdings at 30 September 2026\n\n| Issuer ID | Company / security | Sector | Issuer domicile | Fund weight | Issuer ESG | Restricted activity tags |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ISS-019 | Everfield Renewables | Utilities | Denmark | 8.00% | 92 | None |\n| ISS-020 | Greenhaven Water | Utilities | United Kingdom | 7.00% | 90 | None |\n| ISS-026 | Marisol Solar | Utilities | Spain | 6.00% | 94 | None |\n| ISS-027 | Windmere Turbines | Industrials | Denmark | 6.00% | 93 | None |\n| ISS-028 | Cirrus Recycling | Materials | Netherlands | 5.00% | 91 | None |\n| ISS-029 | Veridian Insulation | Industrials | Germany | 5.00% | 88 | None |\n| ISS-030 | Bluefen Software | Technology | Sweden | 5.00% | 90 | None |\n| ISS-031 | Kindwell Diagnostics | Healthcare | United Kingdom | 4.50% | 92 | None |\n| ISS-032 | Meadowline Packaging | Materials | France | 4.50% | 87 | None |\n| ISS-033 | Fairhaven Finance | Financials | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 84 | None |\n| ISS-034 | Suncrest Storage | Technology | South Korea | 4.00% | 89 | None |\n| ISS-035 | Arboris Food Systems | Consumer Staples | Netherlands | 4.00% | 90 | None |\n| ISS-005 | Alderwell Health | Healthcare | United Kingdom | 3.50% | 89 | None |\n| ISS-006 | CobaltGrid Utilities | Utilities | Germany | 3.50% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-008 | Pelagos Logistics | Industrials | Netherlands | 3.50% | 71 | None |\n| ISS-015 | Glasswater Insurance | Financials | Switzerland | 3.00% | 81 | None |\n| ISS-016 | Halcyon Medical | Healthcare | United States | 3.00% | 85 | None |\n| ISS-021 | Oakspire Housing | Real Estate | United Kingdom | 3.00% | 80 | None |\n| ISS-022 | Silvermere Rail | Industrials | United Kingdom | 3.00% | 83 | None |\n| ISS-023 | Auren Mobility | Consumer Discretionary | Germany | 2.50% | 81 | None |\n| ISS-024 | Clearstream Payments | Financials | United States | 2.50% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-025 | Solenne Agriculture | Consumer Staples | France | 2.50% | 86 | None |\n| ISS-001 | LumenArc Systems | Technology | United States | 2.50% | 88 | None |\n| ISS-003 | QuantaBridge Semiconductors | Technology | Taiwan | 2.50% | 75 | None |\n| ISS-010 | Brackenridge Foods | Consumer Staples | United Kingdom | 2.00% | 77 | None |\n\n**Total fund weight: 100.00%.** Issuer domicile is a classification field; it is not a breakdown of revenue exposure. ESG scores in this table assess issuers, while the headline fund ESG score is a separate approved fund assessment.\n\n## Sector allocation\n\n| Sector | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| Utilities | 24.50% |\n| Industrials | 17.50% |\n| Technology | 14.00% |\n| Healthcare | 11.00% |\n| Materials | 9.50% |\n| Financials | 9.50% |\n| Consumer Staples | 8.50% |\n| Real Estate | 3.00% |\n| Consumer Discretionary | 2.50% |\n\n## Restricted-activity exposure\n\n| Activity | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| tobacco | 0.00% |\n| weapons | 0.00% |\n| fossil_fuels | 0.00% |\n\nThe activity calculation includes every position in a tagged issuer, including corporate bonds. A blank activity list means no restricted activity has been identified in the verified synthetic register; missing classifications in other datasets must trigger review. Refer to DOC-POL-001 for assessment rules and DOC-POL-003 for fee treatment.\n', 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-002'},
    {'document_id': 4, 'path': 'documents/DOC-COM-002.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 4, 'title': 'Wrenford Sustainable Leaders Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary', 'document_type': 'manager_commentary', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 2, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-002'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Sustainable Leaders Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-COM-002  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n**Period:** 1 July–30 September 2026  \n**Author:** Theo Calder, Fund Manager\n\n## Infrastructure holdings supported sustainable returns\n\nThe fund returned 4.6% in Q3 against 4.1% for its fictional sustainable benchmark, an arithmetic lead of 0.5 percentage points. The manager attributes the relative improvement primarily to resilient infrastructure and essential-services holdings, without providing a complete quantitative attribution model.\n\nEverfield Renewables reported progress on connecting approved projects, while Greenhaven Water benefited from firmer expectations for regulated investment. Kindwell Diagnostics showed improved demand for its testing systems. Suncrest Storage experienced a weaker period as customers delayed some equipment orders, demonstrating that a sustainability theme does not protect an investment from business-cycle risks.\n\nThe team increased research coverage of recycling and packaging businesses and retained Cirrus Recycling following a review of contract renewal rates. It trimmed a small part of Windmere Turbines after the share price rose faster than the team's earnings assumptions. The September factsheet records the resulting portfolio weights; this commentary gives reasons for the decisions rather than a transaction ledger.\n\nNo tobacco-, weapons- or fossil_fuels-tagged issuer appears in the current holdings register. That finding comes from the approved activity tags, not from assuming that a fund with a sustainable name must satisfy every client's restrictions. The fund's approved ESG score remains 86 under the internal assessment process.\n\nThe team will monitor project execution, access to finance and evidence supporting environmental claims. It will also follow up the open engagement request to Suncrest Storage on recycling controls. The client-facing explanation is that a range of infrastructure and essential-services investments performed well, while some equipment suppliers faced delays. Returns remain uncertain and the fund still carries equity risk.\n\n## Source and interpretation\n\nThe matching period-end factsheet is DOC-FS-002. Commentary explains the manager's view; numeric holdings and fees come from the current structured snapshot. No quantitative security attribution, future return forecast or investment recommendation is supplied.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-002'},
    {'document_id': 5, 'path': 'documents/DOC-FS-003.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 5, 'title': 'Wrenford Emerging Markets Opportunities Fund — Fund Factsheet', 'document_type': 'fund_factsheet', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '2.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 3, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': 28, 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-003'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Emerging Markets Opportunities Fund — Fund Factsheet\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-FS-003  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 2.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## Investment objective and process\nThe fund seeks long-term growth from businesses operating in emerging economies. Research combines company accounts, local distribution models and governance assessment. Financial services, digital platforms and manufacturing are represented, alongside selected materials and consumer businesses. The manager accepts that local currencies and changes in market access can materially affect sterling returns.\n\nThe mandate of the fund itself permits tobacco-related and fossil-fuel businesses. Orient Leaf Products is classified in Consumer Staples but carries the tobacco activity tag. Azura Petrochem carries the fossil_fuels tag. Sector-only screening would therefore miss part of the fund's restricted-activity exposure.\n\n## Principal risks and monitoring\nPolitical developments, capital controls, market liquidity and variable reporting quality can make valuations and exits more difficult. Currency effects can offset local share-price gains. The portfolio's materials companies add commodity-cycle sensitivity. A high risk rating reflects these combined risks within the internal training scale.\n\n## ESG evidence exception\nThe September fund-level ESG review has not been approved, so the current ESG score is unavailable. It must remain null in structured records. Aquila Biotech also lacks a verified issuer-level ESG score, although its identity and activity classification have been verified. The February score of 58 appearing in the archived factsheet must not fill the current gap. A mandate that requires a minimum ESG score needs human review until the current assessment is approved.\n\n## Fund characteristics and performance\n\n| Metric | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fund ID | FND-003 |\n| Manager | Leena Voss |\n| Share class | GBP accumulation |\n| Investment strategy | Emerging-market equities |\n| Benchmark | Wrenford Synthetic Emerging Markets Index |\n| Q3 2026 net return | -2.4% |\n| Q3 benchmark return | 1.1% |\n| Relative return, arithmetic | -3.5 percentage points |\n| Trailing 12-month net return | 3.7% |\n| Management fee, annual | 1.10% |\n| Other ongoing costs, annual | 0.20% |\n| Ongoing charges, annual | 1.30% |\n| Estimated transaction costs, annual | 0.08% |\n| Performance fee | None |\n| Internal risk rating | 6 / 7 |\n| Internal ESG score | Unavailable — September review pending |\n| Holdings coverage | 100% of NAV; 25 securities; no residual cash, derivatives or fund-of-funds positions |\n\nReturns are measured in sterling with income reinvested, net of ongoing charges. Transaction costs are reflected in the fund value. Adviser and platform fees are excluded. All return periods end on 30 September 2026. These simulated values do not describe actual investments.\n\n## Complete holdings at 30 September 2026\n\n| Issuer ID | Company / security | Sector | Issuer domicile | Fund weight | Issuer ESG | Restricted activity tags |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ISS-036 | LotusGate Internet | Technology | India | 8.00% | 64 | None |\n| ISS-037 | JadeRiver Commerce | Consumer Discretionary | China | 7.00% | 59 | None |\n| ISS-038 | Sundara Bank | Financials | India | 6.00% | 67 | None |\n| ISS-039 | Pacifica Chips | Technology | Taiwan | 6.00% | 72 | None |\n| ISS-040 | Kestrel Battery | Industrials | South Korea | 5.00% | 74 | None |\n| ISS-041 | Andes Copper | Materials | Chile | 5.00% | 51 | None |\n| ISS-042 | Cerrado Foods | Consumer Staples | Brazil | 5.00% | 60 | None |\n| ISS-043 | Nusantara Telecom | Telecommunications | Indonesia | 4.50% | 63 | None |\n| ISS-044 | Kalahari Gold | Materials | South Africa | 4.50% | 48 | None |\n| ISS-045 | Saffron Pharma | Healthcare | India | 4.00% | 76 | None |\n| ISS-046 | Mekong Logistics | Industrials | Vietnam | 4.00% | 65 | None |\n| ISS-047 | Bosphorus Textiles | Consumer Discretionary | Turkey | 4.00% | 54 | None |\n| ISS-048 | Azura Petrochem | Energy | Malaysia | 3.50% | 39 | fossil_fuels |\n| ISS-049 | Orient Leaf Products | Consumer Staples | China | 3.50% | 25 | tobacco |\n| ISS-050 | Banyan Microfinance | Financials | India | 3.50% | 71 | None |\n| ISS-051 | Atlas Harbour Ports | Industrials | Morocco | 3.00% | 62 | None |\n| ISS-052 | Selva Utilities | Utilities | Brazil | 3.00% | 58 | None |\n| ISS-053 | CoralPeak Insurance | Financials | Thailand | 3.00% | 68 | None |\n| ISS-054 | Hanbit Networks | Telecommunications | South Korea | 3.00% | 73 | None |\n| ISS-055 | Aquila Biotech | Healthcare | China | 2.50% | Unavailable | None |\n| ISS-056 | Tamarind Consumer | Consumer Staples | India | 2.50% | 69 | None |\n| ISS-057 | Darya Steel | Materials | India | 2.50% | 45 | None |\n| ISS-058 | Indigo Data Services | Technology | India | 2.50% | 78 | None |\n| ISS-003 | QuantaBridge Semiconductors | Technology | Taiwan | 2.50% | 75 | None |\n| ISS-034 | Suncrest Storage | Technology | South Korea | 2.00% | 89 | None |\n\n**Total fund weight: 100.00%.** Issuer domicile is a classification field; it is not a breakdown of revenue exposure. ESG scores in this table assess issuers, while the headline fund ESG score is a separate approved fund assessment.\n\n## Sector allocation\n\n| Sector | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| Technology | 21.00% |\n| Financials | 12.50% |\n| Industrials | 12.00% |\n| Materials | 12.00% |\n| Consumer Discretionary | 11.00% |\n| Consumer Staples | 11.00% |\n| Telecommunications | 7.50% |\n| Healthcare | 6.50% |\n| Energy | 3.50% |\n| Utilities | 3.00% |\n\n## Restricted-activity exposure\n\n| Activity | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| tobacco | 3.50% |\n| weapons | 0.00% |\n| fossil_fuels | 3.50% |\n\nThe activity calculation includes every position in a tagged issuer, including corporate bonds. A blank activity list means no restricted activity has been identified in the verified synthetic register; missing classifications in other datasets must trigger review. Refer to DOC-POL-001 for assessment rules and DOC-POL-003 for fee treatment.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-003'},
    {'document_id': 6, 'path': 'documents/DOC-COM-003.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 6, 'title': 'Wrenford Emerging Markets Opportunities Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary', 'document_type': 'manager_commentary', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 3, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-003'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Emerging Markets Opportunities Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-COM-003  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n**Period:** 1 July–30 September 2026  \n**Author:** Leena Voss, Fund Manager\n\n## Emerging-market shortfall reflected stock selection and currency\n\nThe fund returned -2.4% in Q3 while its fictional emerging-market benchmark returned 1.1%. The resulting arithmetic underperformance was 3.5 percentage points. The manager's explanation is specific to this invented quarter and should not be presented as a description of real financial markets.\n\nJadeRiver Commerce faced slower order growth and higher distribution costs. LotusGate Internet experienced a valuation decline after revenue expectations were revised. The portfolio's materials holdings, including Andes Copper and Kalahari Gold, also weakened as the manager reduced assumptions for commodity demand. Local-currency depreciation against sterling reduced the translated value of some holdings. This document does not quantify the separate contribution of each factor.\n\nPacifica Chips provided some support through stronger production orders, and Saffron Pharma held up relatively well. The positive contributions were insufficient to offset the weaker consumer, digital-platform and materials holdings. The manager kept a diversified spread of businesses rather than increasing a single position to recover losses.\n\nDuring the period, the team reduced JadeRiver Commerce and reviewed reporting quality at Aquila Biotech. The latter's issuer ESG score remains unavailable. The fund-level September ESG assessment is also awaiting approval and must be recorded as null. The archived February score of 58 is a historical assessment, not evidence that the September minimum-score test passes.\n\nThe next quarter's research priorities are cash conversion, currency sensitivity and disclosure quality. For an adviser, a clear explanation is that weaker company performance and currency translation both contributed to the loss, while some semiconductor and healthcare investments offered partial support. There is no reliable numerical breakdown in the available narrative, and no recovery forecast is supported.\n\n## Source and interpretation\n\nThe matching period-end factsheet is DOC-FS-003. Commentary explains the manager's view; numeric holdings and fees come from the current structured snapshot. No quantitative security attribution, future return forecast or investment recommendation is supplied.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-003'},
    {'document_id': 7, 'path': 'documents/DOC-FS-004.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 7, 'title': 'Wrenford UK Equity Income Fund — Fund Factsheet', 'document_type': 'fund_factsheet', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 4, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-004'}, 'content': "# Wrenford UK Equity Income Fund — Fund Factsheet\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-FS-004  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## Investment objective and process\nThe fund aims to grow capital while earning dividends from predominantly UK businesses. The portfolio combines banks, food producers, utilities, service companies and industrial businesses. Analysts assess the affordability of dividends using cash generation, debt commitments and future investment needs. A high indicated yield is not sufficient on its own to justify a holding.\n\nThe GBP accumulation share class reinvests income rather than paying it directly to an investor. The indicative portfolio dividend yield is 3.8% a year at this snapshot. It is a characteristic of the underlying investments, not a guaranteed payment or a forecast of total return. An adviser would need separate arrangements if a client requires regular cash withdrawals.\n\n## Principal risks and monitoring\nUK market concentration can create common exposure to domestic demand and financial conditions. Dividend payments can be reduced or cancelled. Property companies and leveraged businesses may be sensitive to refinancing costs. The strategy permits tobacco, weapons and fossil-fuel issuers, including Redleaf Tobacco and Ironvale Defence, so it may conflict with stricter responsible-investment mandates despite its income objective.\n\n## Portfolio review\nDuring the quarter the manager reassessed dividend cover at Hearthstone Leisure and borrowing costs at Orchardgate Property. No change to a client mandate follows automatically from that work. All restrictions must be tested using current holdings and the client's signed rule set.\n\n## Fund characteristics and performance\n\n| Metric | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fund ID | FND-004 |\n| Manager | Jonas Reed |\n| Share class | GBP accumulation |\n| Investment strategy | UK dividend-paying equities |\n| Benchmark | Wrenford Synthetic UK Dividend Index |\n| Q3 2026 net return | 2.1% |\n| Q3 benchmark return | 2.8% |\n| Relative return, arithmetic | -0.7 percentage points |\n| Trailing 12-month net return | 6.8% |\n| Management fee, annual | 0.60% |\n| Other ongoing costs, annual | 0.15% |\n| Ongoing charges, annual | 0.75% |\n| Estimated transaction costs, annual | 0.08% |\n| Performance fee | None |\n| Internal risk rating | 4 / 7 |\n| Internal ESG score | 67 / 100 |\n| Holdings coverage | 100% of NAV; 25 securities; no residual cash, derivatives or fund-of-funds positions |\n\nReturns are measured in sterling with income reinvested, net of ongoing charges. Transaction costs are reflected in the fund value. Adviser and platform fees are excluded. All return periods end on 30 September 2026. These simulated values do not describe actual investments.\n\n## Complete holdings at 30 September 2026\n\n| Issuer ID | Company / security | Sector | Issuer domicile | Fund weight | Issuer ESG | Restricted activity tags |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ISS-004 | Northhaven Bank | Financials | United Kingdom | 8.00% | 73 | None |\n| ISS-010 | Brackenridge Foods | Consumer Staples | United Kingdom | 7.00% | 77 | None |\n| ISS-014 | Seabrook Telecom | Telecommunications | United Kingdom | 6.00% | 74 | None |\n| ISS-015 | Glasswater Insurance | Financials | Switzerland | 6.00% | 81 | None |\n| ISS-005 | Alderwell Health | Healthcare | United Kingdom | 5.00% | 89 | None |\n| ISS-020 | Greenhaven Water | Utilities | United Kingdom | 5.00% | 90 | None |\n| ISS-021 | Oakspire Housing | Real Estate | United Kingdom | 5.00% | 80 | None |\n| ISS-022 | Silvermere Rail | Industrials | United Kingdom | 4.50% | 83 | None |\n| ISS-060 | Westbrook Engineering | Industrials | United Kingdom | 4.50% | 72 | None |\n| ISS-061 | Hearthstone Leisure | Consumer Discretionary | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 66 | None |\n| ISS-062 | Bramblewick Assurance | Financials | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 75 | None |\n| ISS-063 | Riverton Grocers | Consumer Staples | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 79 | None |\n| ISS-064 | Orchardgate Property | Real Estate | United Kingdom | 3.50% | 74 | None |\n| ISS-065 | Kingswell Engineering | Industrials | United Kingdom | 3.50% | 76 | None |\n| ISS-066 | Moorland Gas | Energy | United Kingdom | 3.50% | 46 | fossil_fuels |\n| ISS-067 | Chalkwell Cement | Materials | United Kingdom | 3.00% | 53 | None |\n| ISS-068 | Fenbridge Services | Industrials | United Kingdom | 3.00% | 77 | None |\n| ISS-011 | Asterion Energy | Energy | United Kingdom | 3.00% | 42 | fossil_fuels |\n| ISS-012 | Redleaf Tobacco | Tobacco | United Kingdom | 3.00% | 22 | tobacco |\n| ISS-013 | Ironvale Defence | Weapons | United States | 2.50% | 35 | weapons |\n| ISS-017 | Norwyn Industrials | Industrials | Germany | 2.50% | 68 | None |\n| ISS-018 | Stoneford Materials | Materials | Australia | 2.50% | 56 | None |\n| ISS-006 | CobaltGrid Utilities | Utilities | Germany | 2.50% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-059 | Aquila Bio Technologies | Healthcare | United Kingdom | 2.50% | 79 | None |\n| ISS-016 | Halcyon Medical | Healthcare | United States | 2.00% | 85 | None |\n\n**Total fund weight: 100.00%.** Issuer domicile is a classification field; it is not a breakdown of revenue exposure. ESG scores in this table assess issuers, while the headline fund ESG score is a separate approved fund assessment.\n\n## Sector allocation\n\n| Sector | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| Financials | 18.00% |\n| Industrials | 18.00% |\n| Consumer Staples | 11.00% |\n| Healthcare | 9.50% |\n| Real Estate | 8.50% |\n| Utilities | 7.50% |\n| Energy | 6.50% |\n| Telecommunications | 6.00% |\n| Materials | 5.50% |\n| Consumer Discretionary | 4.00% |\n| Tobacco | 3.00% |\n| Weapons | 2.50% |\n\n## Restricted-activity exposure\n\n| Activity | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| tobacco | 3.00% |\n| weapons | 2.50% |\n| fossil_fuels | 6.50% |\n\nThe activity calculation includes every position in a tagged issuer, including corporate bonds. A blank activity list means no restricted activity has been identified in the verified synthetic register; missing classifications in other datasets must trigger review. Refer to DOC-POL-001 for assessment rules and DOC-POL-003 for fee treatment.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-004'},
    {'document_id': 8, 'path': 'documents/DOC-COM-004.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 8, 'title': 'Wrenford UK Equity Income Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary', 'document_type': 'manager_commentary', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 4, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-004'}, 'content': "# Wrenford UK Equity Income Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-COM-004  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n**Period:** 1 July–30 September 2026  \n**Author:** Jonas Reed, Fund Manager\n\n## Dividend resilience accompanied modest relative lag\n\nThe fund returned 2.1% in Q3 versus 2.8% for its fictional dividend benchmark, an arithmetic shortfall of 0.7 percentage points. Reinvested dividends formed part of the fund's total return. The indicative 3.8% portfolio dividend yield is an annualised snapshot measure and must not be added to the quarter's return.\n\nNorthhaven Bank and Brackenridge Foods provided resilient earnings and dividend cover. Greenhaven Water also supported results as its regulated investment programme progressed. Hearthstone Leisure weakened following softer customer demand, and Orchardgate Property was affected by higher expected refinancing costs. The manager describes these as principal performance drivers, not a complete numerical attribution.\n\nThe team reduced exposure to Hearthstone Leisure and added selectively to Silvermere Rail following contract reviews. It kept Redleaf Tobacco within the fund's investment universe because the fund itself has no tobacco exclusion. An adviser reviewing the Marlow mandate must therefore calculate the portfolio's indirect tobacco exposure rather than assume an income strategy is automatically acceptable.\n\nThe accumulation share class reinvests income. A client requiring cash payments would need a distribution share class or a withdrawal arrangement assessed separately. Neither the screening report nor this commentary verifies a withdrawal plan, tax treatment or the client's capacity for loss.\n\nResearch priorities remain dividend cover, borrowing costs and the quality of free cash flow. The intended client explanation is that the fund made a positive return but trailed its comparator, with robust bank and food holdings offset by weaker leisure and property investments. A dividend objective does not guarantee capital stability, and individual client restrictions remain binding.\n\n## Source and interpretation\n\nThe matching period-end factsheet is DOC-FS-004. Commentary explains the manager's view; numeric holdings and fees come from the current structured snapshot. No quantitative security attribution, future return forecast or investment recommendation is supplied.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-004'},
    {'document_id': 9, 'path': 'documents/DOC-FS-005.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 9, 'title': 'Wrenford Technology Innovation Fund — Fund Factsheet', 'document_type': 'fund_factsheet', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 5, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-005'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Technology Innovation Fund — Fund Factsheet\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-FS-005  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## Investment objective and process\nThe fund seeks long-term growth from computing infrastructure, software, semiconductors and businesses supporting digital adoption. The manager assesses revenue quality, research spending, customer concentration and the ability to turn sales growth into cash. The portfolio includes several smaller positions alongside established companies, creating a mix of mature and developing business models.\n\nTechnology is the dominant sector. Payments, communications and industrial automation holdings provide some variation in business activity, but they do not remove the fund's sensitivity to technology spending. LumenArc Systems is also held by other Wrenford funds. Client-level concentration should aggregate exposures using its canonical issuer ID rather than count slightly different company names as separate businesses.\n\n## Principal risks and monitoring\nHigh valuations can magnify losses when earnings expectations change. Rapid technical change can shorten product lives, and export restrictions can disrupt semiconductor supply chains. The manager monitors dependence on a few large customers and the funding needs of companies that have not established stable free cash flow. An internal risk rating of 6 indicates a relatively high-risk strategy within this training dataset.\n\n## ESG and fees\nThe fund's internal ESG score of 66 reflects uneven disclosure and resource-intensive computing activities. It is below some clients' minimum threshold even though several individual holdings score well. This fund also has the highest ongoing charge in the six-fund range. Strong past performance alone does not resolve either a mandate breach or a fee concern.\n\n## Fund characteristics and performance\n\n| Metric | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fund ID | FND-005 |\n| Manager | Nadia Rowan |\n| Share class | GBP accumulation |\n| Investment strategy | Global technology and digital infrastructure equities |\n| Benchmark | Wrenford Synthetic Digital Innovation Index |\n| Q3 2026 net return | 7.8% |\n| Q3 benchmark return | 8.6% |\n| Relative return, arithmetic | -0.8 percentage points |\n| Trailing 12-month net return | 18.3% |\n| Management fee, annual | 1.20% |\n| Other ongoing costs, annual | 0.15% |\n| Ongoing charges, annual | 1.35% |\n| Estimated transaction costs, annual | 0.08% |\n| Performance fee | None |\n| Internal risk rating | 6 / 7 |\n| Internal ESG score | 66 / 100 |\n| Holdings coverage | 100% of NAV; 25 securities; no residual cash, derivatives or fund-of-funds positions |\n\nReturns are measured in sterling with income reinvested, net of ongoing charges. Transaction costs are reflected in the fund value. Adviser and platform fees are excluded. All return periods end on 30 September 2026. These simulated values do not describe actual investments.\n\n## Complete holdings at 30 September 2026\n\n| Issuer ID | Company / security | Sector | Issuer domicile | Fund weight | Issuer ESG | Restricted activity tags |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ISS-001 | LumenArc Systems | Technology | United States | 8.00% | 88 | None |\n| ISS-002 | Bellmere Devices | Technology | United States | 7.00% | 78 | None |\n| ISS-003 | QuantaBridge Semiconductors | Technology | Taiwan | 6.00% | 75 | None |\n| ISS-009 | Meridian Cloudworks | Technology | United States | 6.00% | 84 | None |\n| ISS-069 | Deepwell Analytics | Technology | United States | 5.00% | 80 | None |\n| ISS-070 | Nexora Robotics | Technology | Japan | 5.00% | 76 | None |\n| ISS-071 | PrismForge AI | Technology | United States | 5.00% | 69 | None |\n| ISS-072 | HelioCore Networks | Technology | United States | 4.50% | 72 | None |\n| ISS-073 | VectorNest Security | Technology | Israel | 4.50% | 74 | None |\n| ISS-074 | Tessera Design | Technology | United States | 4.00% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-075 | Ionvale Computing | Technology | United States | 4.00% | 65 | None |\n| ISS-076 | AsterCloud Hosting | Technology | United States | 4.00% | 67 | None |\n| ISS-077 | PixelHarbour Media | Telecommunications | United States | 3.50% | 71 | None |\n| ISS-078 | CobaltCircuit Components | Technology | South Korea | 3.50% | 70 | None |\n| ISS-079 | FluxEdge Software | Technology | United States | 3.50% | 73 | None |\n| ISS-080 | OrionPath Automation | Industrials | Japan | 3.00% | 77 | None |\n| ISS-081 | NanoSpring Instruments | Technology | United States | 3.00% | 68 | None |\n| ISS-082 | AstraMesh Connectivity | Technology | Sweden | 3.00% | 81 | None |\n| ISS-083 | VelaStack Platforms | Technology | United States | 3.00% | 66 | None |\n| ISS-084 | Copperfin Digital | Financials | United States | 2.50% | 75 | None |\n| ISS-024 | Clearstream Payments | Financials | United States | 2.50% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-030 | Bluefen Software | Technology | Sweden | 2.50% | 90 | None |\n| ISS-034 | Suncrest Storage | Technology | South Korea | 2.50% | 89 | None |\n| ISS-039 | Pacifica Chips | Technology | Taiwan | 2.50% | 72 | None |\n| ISS-058 | Indigo Data Services | Technology | India | 2.00% | 78 | None |\n\n**Total fund weight: 100.00%.** Issuer domicile is a classification field; it is not a breakdown of revenue exposure. ESG scores in this table assess issuers, while the headline fund ESG score is a separate approved fund assessment.\n\n## Sector allocation\n\n| Sector | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| Technology | 88.50% |\n| Financials | 5.00% |\n| Telecommunications | 3.50% |\n| Industrials | 3.00% |\n\n## Restricted-activity exposure\n\n| Activity | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| tobacco | 0.00% |\n| weapons | 0.00% |\n| fossil_fuels | 0.00% |\n\nThe activity calculation includes every position in a tagged issuer, including corporate bonds. A blank activity list means no restricted activity has been identified in the verified synthetic register; missing classifications in other datasets must trigger review. Refer to DOC-POL-001 for assessment rules and DOC-POL-003 for fee treatment.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-005'},
    {'document_id': 10, 'path': 'documents/DOC-COM-005.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 10, 'title': 'Wrenford Technology Innovation Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary', 'document_type': 'manager_commentary', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 5, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-005'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Technology Innovation Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-COM-005  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n**Period:** 1 July–30 September 2026  \n**Author:** Nadia Rowan, Fund Manager\n\n## Strong technology gains fell short of a faster benchmark\n\nThe fund returned 7.8% in Q3 compared with 8.6% for its fictional innovation benchmark, an arithmetic shortfall of 0.8 percentage points despite a strong positive absolute return. Both facts matter: making money and beating a benchmark are different comparisons.\n\nLumenArc Systems, QuantaBridge Semiconductors and Meridian Cloudworks benefited from demand for computing infrastructure. PrismForge AI supported returns as customers increased adoption of its tools. Ionvale Computing and AsterCloud Hosting were weaker because infrastructure expenditure rose faster than expected cash generation. The portfolio's smaller digital businesses did not match the strongest benchmark constituents.\n\nThe manager trimmed Bellmere Devices after reviewing its valuation and added modestly to VectorNest Security. The team also challenged providers on customer concentration and electricity requirements. These observations do not establish an independently verified sustainability outcome, and the fund's approved ESG score remains 66.\n\nThe technology allocation is large even after including payments and industrial automation businesses. LumenArc Systems is shared with the Global Quality fund. In the Hartwell portfolio, combining 60% Technology Innovation with 40% Global Quality produces an 8.0% look-through exposure to that issuer because both funds hold it at 8.0%. Different spellings in the two feeds must not dilute that calculation.\n\nThe manager will monitor order durability, margins and funding needs. Past gains do not justify waiving Hartwell's 6% issuer limit or 40% technology-sector limit. A plain-language update should explain that the fund rose strongly but lagged an even stronger comparator, while its concentrated strategy and relatively high fee remain relevant risks.\n\n## Source and interpretation\n\nThe matching period-end factsheet is DOC-FS-005. Commentary explains the manager's view; numeric holdings and fees come from the current structured snapshot. No quantitative security attribution, future return forecast or investment recommendation is supplied.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-005'},
    {'document_id': 11, 'path': 'documents/DOC-FS-006.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 11, 'title': 'Wrenford Global Corporate Bond Fund — Fund Factsheet', 'document_type': 'fund_factsheet', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 6, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-006'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Global Corporate Bond Fund — Fund Factsheet\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-FS-006  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## Investment objective and process\nThe fund seeks income and moderate capital growth from investment-grade corporate bonds. Credit analysts review issuer cash flow, leverage, debt seniority and refinancing requirements. All 25 positions in this snapshot are bonds; the company names identify borrowers rather than equity ownership. Several borrowers also appear in the equity funds, so combined issuer exposure includes both bonds and shares.\n\nEach position is a fictional sterling-denominated bond. The GBP-hedged strategy designation allows non-sterling issues in other periods, but this snapshot contains none and has no open currency hedge. The portfolio's indicative modified duration is 4.6 years and yield to maturity is 4.2% a year. Yield to maturity is a valuation measure with assumptions about payments and reinvestment; it is not a promised investor return.\n\n## Principal risks and monitoring\nBond values can fall when market yields rise or when an issuer's credit quality weakens. Investment-grade status does not eliminate default or liquidity risk. The duration measure gives an approximate indication of sensitivity to a small parallel change in yields and does not capture every possible curve movement. Credit spread widening can reduce bond prices even when the broader interest-rate environment is unchanged.\n\n## Restrictions and evidence\nThe fund excludes tobacco- and weapons-tagged issuers under its internal strategy. The current register identifies neither activity in its holdings. The manager monitors rating changes and covenant information. Risk rating 3 describes a lower risk band than the equity strategies in this pack, but capital is still at risk and client suitability requires a separate professional assessment.\n\n## Fund characteristics and performance\n\n| Metric | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fund ID | FND-006 |\n| Manager | Elias Mercer |\n| Share class | GBP accumulation |\n| Investment strategy | Developed-market investment-grade corporate bonds |\n| Benchmark | Wrenford Synthetic Corporate Bond Index GBP Hedged |\n| Q3 2026 net return | 1.5% |\n| Q3 benchmark return | 1.2% |\n| Relative return, arithmetic | +0.3 percentage points |\n| Trailing 12-month net return | 4.9% |\n| Management fee, annual | 0.40% |\n| Other ongoing costs, annual | 0.15% |\n| Ongoing charges, annual | 0.55% |\n| Estimated transaction costs, annual | 0.04% |\n| Performance fee | None |\n| Internal risk rating | 3 / 7 |\n| Internal ESG score | 74 / 100 |\n| Holdings coverage | 100% of NAV; 25 securities; no residual cash, derivatives or fund-of-funds positions |\n\nReturns are measured in sterling with income reinvested, net of ongoing charges. Transaction costs are reflected in the fund value. Adviser and platform fees are excluded. All return periods end on 30 September 2026. These simulated values do not describe actual investments.\n\n## Complete holdings at 30 September 2026\n\n| Issuer ID | Company / security | Sector | Issuer domicile | Fund weight | Issuer ESG | Restricted activity tags |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ISS-004 | Northhaven Bank 4.25% 2031 | Financials | United Kingdom | 8.00% | 73 | None |\n| ISS-006 | CobaltGrid Utilities 4.50% 2032 | Utilities | Germany | 7.00% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-014 | Seabrook Telecom 4.75% 2033 | Telecommunications | United Kingdom | 6.00% | 74 | None |\n| ISS-015 | Glasswater Insurance 5.00% 2034 | Financials | Switzerland | 6.00% | 81 | None |\n| ISS-020 | Greenhaven Water 4.00% 2035 | Utilities | United Kingdom | 5.00% | 90 | None |\n| ISS-005 | Alderwell Health 4.25% 2030 | Healthcare | United Kingdom | 5.00% | 89 | None |\n| ISS-010 | Brackenridge Foods 4.50% 2031 | Consumer Staples | United Kingdom | 5.00% | 77 | None |\n| ISS-008 | Pelagos Logistics 4.75% 2032 | Industrials | Netherlands | 4.50% | 71 | None |\n| ISS-017 | Norwyn Industrials 5.00% 2033 | Industrials | Germany | 4.50% | 68 | None |\n| ISS-021 | Oakspire Housing 4.00% 2034 | Real Estate | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 80 | None |\n| ISS-022 | Silvermere Rail 4.25% 2035 | Industrials | United Kingdom | 4.00% | 83 | None |\n| ISS-023 | Auren Mobility 4.50% 2030 | Consumer Discretionary | Germany | 4.00% | 81 | None |\n| ISS-024 | Clearstream Payments 4.75% 2031 | Financials | United States | 3.50% | 82 | None |\n| ISS-025 | Solenne Agriculture 5.00% 2032 | Consumer Staples | France | 3.50% | 86 | None |\n| ISS-019 | Everfield Renewables 4.00% 2033 | Utilities | Denmark | 3.50% | 92 | None |\n| ISS-001 | LumenArc Systems 4.25% 2034 | Technology | United States | 3.00% | 88 | None |\n| ISS-009 | Meridian Cloudworks 4.50% 2035 | Technology | United States | 3.00% | 84 | None |\n| ISS-016 | Halcyon Medical 4.75% 2030 | Healthcare | United States | 3.00% | 85 | None |\n| ISS-060 | Westbrook Engineering 5.00% 2031 | Industrials | United Kingdom | 3.00% | 72 | None |\n| ISS-062 | Bramblewick Assurance 4.00% 2032 | Financials | United Kingdom | 2.50% | 75 | None |\n| ISS-063 | Riverton Grocers 4.25% 2033 | Consumer Staples | United Kingdom | 2.50% | 79 | None |\n| ISS-064 | Orchardgate Property 4.50% 2034 | Real Estate | United Kingdom | 2.50% | 74 | None |\n| ISS-086 | AuroraLedger Services 4.75% 2035 | Technology | United States | 2.50% | 72 | None |\n| ISS-087 | Pinehaven Credit 5.00% 2030 | Financials | United Kingdom | 2.50% | 78 | None |\n| ISS-088 | Elmshore Utilities 4.00% 2031 | Utilities | France | 2.00% | 79 | None |\n\n**Total fund weight: 100.00%.** Issuer domicile is a classification field; it is not a breakdown of revenue exposure. ESG scores in this table assess issuers, while the headline fund ESG score is a separate approved fund assessment.\n\n## Sector allocation\n\n| Sector | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| Financials | 22.50% |\n| Utilities | 17.50% |\n| Industrials | 16.00% |\n| Consumer Staples | 11.00% |\n| Technology | 8.50% |\n| Healthcare | 8.00% |\n| Real Estate | 6.50% |\n| Telecommunications | 6.00% |\n| Consumer Discretionary | 4.00% |\n\n## Restricted-activity exposure\n\n| Activity | Fund weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| tobacco | 0.00% |\n| weapons | 0.00% |\n| fossil_fuels | 0.00% |\n\nThe activity calculation includes every position in a tagged issuer, including corporate bonds. A blank activity list means no restricted activity has been identified in the verified synthetic register; missing classifications in other datasets must trigger review. Refer to DOC-POL-001 for assessment rules and DOC-POL-003 for fee treatment.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-FS-006'},
    {'document_id': 12, 'path': 'documents/DOC-COM-006.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 12, 'title': 'Wrenford Global Corporate Bond Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary', 'document_type': 'manager_commentary', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': 6, 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-006'}, 'content': "# Wrenford Global Corporate Bond Fund — Q3 2026 Manager Commentary\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-COM-006  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n**Period:** 1 July–30 September 2026  \n**Author:** Elias Mercer, Fund Manager\n\n## Credit income and stable valuations supported bond returns\n\nThe fund returned 1.5% in Q3 against 1.2% for its fictional GBP-hedged corporate-bond benchmark, an arithmetic lead of 0.3 percentage points. Coupon accrual and a modest improvement in selected credit valuations supported the result. This narrative does not separate income return from price return numerically.\n\nNorthhaven Bank and Glasswater Insurance benefited from stable credit assessments. Utility borrowers including CobaltGrid Utilities and Greenhaven Water retained predictable financing profiles in the manager's review. Norwyn Industrials was monitored more closely following slower orders, although its bond remained within the investment-grade universe in this snapshot.\n\nThe portfolio contains sterling-denominated bonds and had no open currency hedge at the reporting date. The fund's GBP-hedged strategy designation permits hedged non-sterling holdings in other periods. No currency-hedging contribution is claimed for this particular quarter. The current 4.6-year duration indicates that material movements in market yields could still change valuations.\n\nThe manager rolled proceeds from a maturing issue into a new fictional investment-grade security and kept the portfolio diversified across 25 borrowers. The period-end holdings table shows the resulting bond positions. Tobacco and weapons activity exclusions were maintained, and the approved fund-level ESG score remains 74.\n\nThe next review will focus on refinancing schedules, downgrade risk and liquidity during stressed trading. For a client, the key message is that interest income and steadier credit valuations supported a modest gain, but corporate bonds can still lose value. The 4.2% yield-to-maturity figure is not a promised return, and a lower risk band does not constitute a suitability approval.\n\n## Source and interpretation\n\nThe matching period-end factsheet is DOC-FS-006. Commentary explains the manager's view; numeric holdings and fees come from the current structured snapshot. No quantitative security attribution, future return forecast or investment recommendation is supplied.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-COM-006'},
    {'document_id': 13, 'path': 'documents/DOC-MND-001.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 13, 'title': 'Haven Cancer Support Responsible Growth Mandate', 'document_type': 'client_mandate', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': 1, 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-001'}, 'content': "# Haven Cancer Support Responsible Growth Mandate\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-MND-001  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Purpose and authority\n\nHaven Cancer Support Trust reserves its investment assets for future programme expenditure and seeks responsible long-term growth. Its trustees prohibit all indirect investment in tobacco and weapons issuers because those activities conflict with the charity's purpose. Diversification limits apply across the entire portfolio, including exposure through more than one fund. The signed mandate takes priority over a fund manager's broader investment permissions.\n\n**Client:** Haven Cancer Support Trust  \n**Mandate ID:** MND-001  \n**Effective:** 2026-07-01  \n**Next scheduled review:** 2027-07-01\n\n## 2. Calculation scope\n\nEvery percentage exposure is a percentage of total client portfolio value. For each fund position, multiply its portfolio weight by the issuer or sector weight in the fund and divide by 100. Sum contributions across all funds. Issuer limits aggregate equity and debt issued by the same canonical issuer. No equity-only exemption applies. The register's activity tags define tobacco and weapons exposure; the sector name alone is insufficient.\n\n## 3. Binding investment rules\n\n### 3.1. RUL-01-01 — activity_exclusion\n\nExposure to issuers tagged **tobacco** must equal 0.00% of the total portfolio. No de minimis exemption is permitted.\n\n### 3.2. RUL-01-02 — activity_exclusion\n\nExposure to issuers tagged **weapons** must equal 0.00% of the total portfolio. No de minimis exemption is permitted.\n\n### 3.3. RUL-01-03 — company_exposure\n\nAggregate exposure to **LumenArc Systems (ISS-001)** must not exceed 5.00% of the total portfolio, including equity and corporate-bond positions.\n\n### 3.4. RUL-01-04 — sector_exposure\n\nExposure to the **Technology** sector must not exceed 25.00% of the total portfolio.\n\n### 3.5. RUL-01-05 — minimum_esg_score\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have a current approved internal ESG score of at least 70/100. This is a per-fund minimum, not a weighted-average test.\n\n### 3.6. RUL-01-06 — maximum_risk_rating\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have an internal risk rating no greater than 5/7. Ratings must not be averaged to bypass this limit.\n\n## 4. Evidence, escalation and amendments\n\nThe inclusive operators apply as written: exposure exactly at a maximum passes, and a score exactly at a minimum passes. Calculations retain full precision until comparison; display rounding must not change the outcome. Missing, stale or unresolved evidence triggers REVIEW for the affected rule. A proven breach remains a BREACH even when another rule needs review.\n\nAny breach must be referred to compliance under DOC-POL-004. A portfolio manager may propose changes but cannot retrospectively waive a result. Amendments require a new approved mandate version and a recorded effective date. The assistant may explain rules and calculations; it must not declare final investment suitability or amend this mandate.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-001'},
    {'document_id': 14, 'path': 'documents/DOC-MND-002.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 14, 'title': 'Willow Community Responsible Investment Mandate', 'document_type': 'client_mandate', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': 2, 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-002'}, 'content': "# Willow Community Responsible Investment Mandate\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-MND-002  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Purpose and authority\n\nWillow Community Foundation invests a long-term reserve to support local health, housing and education projects. Its trustees require responsible investment controls alongside exposure diversification. The mandate excludes tobacco and weapons throughout the portfolio. A sustainable fund name is insufficient evidence: current holdings, activity classifications and the approved fund assessments must support every screening result.\n\n**Client:** Willow Community Foundation  \n**Mandate ID:** MND-002  \n**Effective:** 2026-07-01  \n**Next scheduled review:** 2027-07-01\n\n## 2. Calculation scope\n\nEvery percentage exposure is a percentage of total client portfolio value. For each fund position, multiply its portfolio weight by the issuer or sector weight in the fund and divide by 100. Sum contributions across all funds. Issuer limits aggregate equity and debt issued by the same canonical issuer. No equity-only exemption applies. The register's activity tags define tobacco and weapons exposure; the sector name alone is insufficient.\n\n## 3. Binding investment rules\n\n### 3.1. RUL-02-01 — activity_exclusion\n\nExposure to issuers tagged **tobacco** must equal 0.00% of the total portfolio. No de minimis exemption is permitted.\n\n### 3.2. RUL-02-02 — activity_exclusion\n\nExposure to issuers tagged **weapons** must equal 0.00% of the total portfolio. No de minimis exemption is permitted.\n\n### 3.3. RUL-02-03 — company_exposure\n\nAggregate exposure to **LumenArc Systems (ISS-001)** must not exceed 5.00% of the total portfolio, including equity and corporate-bond positions.\n\n### 3.4. RUL-02-04 — sector_exposure\n\nExposure to the **Technology** sector must not exceed 25.00% of the total portfolio.\n\n### 3.5. RUL-02-05 — minimum_esg_score\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have a current approved internal ESG score of at least 70/100. This is a per-fund minimum, not a weighted-average test.\n\n### 3.6. RUL-02-06 — maximum_risk_rating\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have an internal risk rating no greater than 5/7. Ratings must not be averaged to bypass this limit.\n\n## 4. Evidence, escalation and amendments\n\nThe inclusive operators apply as written: exposure exactly at a maximum passes, and a score exactly at a minimum passes. Calculations retain full precision until comparison; display rounding must not change the outcome. Missing, stale or unresolved evidence triggers REVIEW for the affected rule. A proven breach remains a BREACH even when another rule needs review.\n\nAny breach must be referred to compliance under DOC-POL-004. A portfolio manager may propose changes but cannot retrospectively waive a result. Amendments require a new approved mandate version and a recorded effective date. The assistant may explain rules and calculations; it must not declare final investment suitability or amend this mandate.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-002'},
    {'document_id': 15, 'path': 'documents/DOC-MND-003.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 15, 'title': 'Hartwell Growth and Concentration Mandate', 'document_type': 'client_mandate', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': 3, 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-003'}, 'content': "# Hartwell Growth and Concentration Mandate\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-MND-003  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Purpose and authority\n\nSofia Hartwell has requested a growth-oriented research portfolio subject to explicit concentration controls. This mandate permits higher-risk equity strategies but limits dependence on one issuer or the technology sector. These limits are binding even after strong performance. The mandate records allocation restrictions only and does not establish that the account is suitable for the client's complete financial circumstances.\n\n**Client:** Sofia Hartwell  \n**Mandate ID:** MND-003  \n**Effective:** 2026-07-01  \n**Next scheduled review:** 2027-07-01\n\n## 2. Calculation scope\n\nEvery percentage exposure is a percentage of total client portfolio value. For each fund position, multiply its portfolio weight by the issuer or sector weight in the fund and divide by 100. Sum contributions across all funds. Issuer limits aggregate equity and debt issued by the same canonical issuer. No equity-only exemption applies. The register's activity tags define tobacco and weapons exposure; the sector name alone is insufficient.\n\n## 3. Binding investment rules\n\n### 3.1. RUL-03-01 — company_exposure\n\nAggregate exposure to **LumenArc Systems (ISS-001)** must not exceed 6.00% of the total portfolio, including equity and corporate-bond positions.\n\n### 3.2. RUL-03-02 — sector_exposure\n\nExposure to the **Technology** sector must not exceed 40.00% of the total portfolio.\n\n### 3.3. RUL-03-03 — maximum_risk_rating\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have an internal risk rating no greater than 6/7. Ratings must not be averaged to bypass this limit.\n\n### 3.4. RUL-03-04 — maximum_fund_weight\n\nNo individual fund may exceed 65.00% of total portfolio value.\n\n## 4. Evidence, escalation and amendments\n\nThe inclusive operators apply as written: exposure exactly at a maximum passes, and a score exactly at a minimum passes. Calculations retain full precision until comparison; display rounding must not change the outcome. Missing, stale or unresolved evidence triggers REVIEW for the affected rule. A proven breach remains a BREACH even when another rule needs review.\n\nAny breach must be referred to compliance under DOC-POL-004. A portfolio manager may propose changes but cannot retrospectively waive a result. Amendments require a new approved mandate version and a recorded effective date. The assistant may explain rules and calculations; it must not declare final investment suitability or amend this mandate.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-003'},
    {'document_id': 16, 'path': 'documents/DOC-MND-004.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 16, 'title': 'Marlow Retirement Allocation Mandate', 'document_type': 'client_mandate', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': 4, 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-004'}, 'content': "# Marlow Retirement Allocation Mandate\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-MND-004  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Purpose and authority\n\nEvan Marlow's retirement allocation combines corporate bonds and dividend-oriented equities. The mandate sets a minimum bond allocation and restricts higher-risk funds. Limited tobacco and weapons exposures are permitted within specified portfolio caps. The account uses accumulation share classes: the mandate does not promise regular cash distributions or approve a withdrawal schedule.\n\n**Client:** Evan Marlow  \n**Mandate ID:** MND-004  \n**Effective:** 2026-07-01  \n**Next scheduled review:** 2027-07-01\n\n## 2. Calculation scope\n\nEvery percentage exposure is a percentage of total client portfolio value. For each fund position, multiply its portfolio weight by the issuer or sector weight in the fund and divide by 100. Sum contributions across all funds. Issuer limits aggregate equity and debt issued by the same canonical issuer. No equity-only exemption applies. The register's activity tags define tobacco and weapons exposure; the sector name alone is insufficient.\n\n## 3. Binding investment rules\n\n### 3.1. RUL-04-01 — activity_exposure\n\nExposure to issuers tagged **tobacco** must not exceed 2.00% of the total portfolio.\n\n### 3.2. RUL-04-02 — activity_exposure\n\nExposure to issuers tagged **weapons** must not exceed 2.00% of the total portfolio.\n\n### 3.3. RUL-04-03 — sector_exposure\n\nExposure to the **Technology** sector must not exceed 20.00% of the total portfolio.\n\n### 3.4. RUL-04-04 — minimum_esg_score\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have a current approved internal ESG score of at least 60/100. This is a per-fund minimum, not a weighted-average test.\n\n### 3.5. RUL-04-05 — maximum_risk_rating\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have an internal risk rating no greater than 4/7. Ratings must not be averaged to bypass this limit.\n\n### 3.6. RUL-04-06 — asset_class_exposure\n\nFunds classified as **fixed_income** must together represent at least 50.00% of total portfolio value.\n\n## 4. Evidence, escalation and amendments\n\nThe inclusive operators apply as written: exposure exactly at a maximum passes, and a score exactly at a minimum passes. Calculations retain full precision until comparison; display rounding must not change the outcome. Missing, stale or unresolved evidence triggers REVIEW for the affected rule. A proven breach remains a BREACH even when another rule needs review.\n\nAny breach must be referred to compliance under DOC-POL-004. A portfolio manager may propose changes but cannot retrospectively waive a result. Amendments require a new approved mandate version and a recorded effective date. The assistant may explain rules and calculations; it must not declare final investment suitability or amend this mandate.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-004'},
    {'document_id': 17, 'path': 'documents/DOC-MND-005.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 17, 'title': 'Ashcombe Research and Evidence Mandate', 'document_type': 'client_mandate', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': 5, 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-005'}, 'content': "# Ashcombe Research and Evidence Mandate\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-MND-005  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Purpose and authority\n\nAshcombe Research Endowment uses a diversified portfolio to support future grant commitments. Its investment committee permits emerging-market holdings but requires a current minimum ESG assessment for every selected fund. An unavailable score is an evidence exception. It must not be treated as a zero score, substituted with a historical score, or silently interpreted as satisfying the threshold.\n\n**Client:** Ashcombe Research Endowment  \n**Mandate ID:** MND-005  \n**Effective:** 2026-07-01  \n**Next scheduled review:** 2027-07-01\n\n## 2. Calculation scope\n\nEvery percentage exposure is a percentage of total client portfolio value. For each fund position, multiply its portfolio weight by the issuer or sector weight in the fund and divide by 100. Sum contributions across all funds. Issuer limits aggregate equity and debt issued by the same canonical issuer. No equity-only exemption applies. The register's activity tags define tobacco and weapons exposure; the sector name alone is insufficient.\n\n## 3. Binding investment rules\n\n### 3.1. RUL-05-01 — minimum_esg_score\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have a current approved internal ESG score of at least 65/100. This is a per-fund minimum, not a weighted-average test.\n\n### 3.2. RUL-05-02 — maximum_risk_rating\n\nEvery fund held at a positive weight must have an internal risk rating no greater than 6/7. Ratings must not be averaged to bypass this limit.\n\n### 3.3. RUL-05-03 — maximum_fund_weight\n\nNo individual fund may exceed 50.00% of total portfolio value.\n\n### 3.4. RUL-05-04 — company_exposure\n\nAggregate exposure to **LumenArc Systems (ISS-001)** must not exceed 5.00% of the total portfolio, including equity and corporate-bond positions.\n\n## 4. Evidence, escalation and amendments\n\nThe inclusive operators apply as written: exposure exactly at a maximum passes, and a score exactly at a minimum passes. Calculations retain full precision until comparison; display rounding must not change the outcome. Missing, stale or unresolved evidence triggers REVIEW for the affected rule. A proven breach remains a BREACH even when another rule needs review.\n\nAny breach must be referred to compliance under DOC-POL-004. A portfolio manager may propose changes but cannot retrospectively waive a result. Amendments require a new approved mandate version and a recorded effective date. The assistant may explain rules and calculations; it must not declare final investment suitability or amend this mandate.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-MND-005'},
    {'document_id': 18, 'path': 'documents/DOC-POL-001.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 18, 'title': 'ESG Assessment and Restricted-Activity Policy', 'document_type': 'esg_policy', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-001'}, 'content': "# ESG Assessment and Restricted-Activity Policy\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-POL-001  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Purpose and ownership\nWrenford's research team maintains internal ESG assessments to support investment analysis and mandate screening. Scores use a fictional 0–100 scale, where higher values reflect stronger evidence across environmental management, social practices and governance. They are internal research measures rather than external ratings or regulatory classifications. The research lead approves methodology changes; compliance owns restriction interpretation.\n\n## 2. Issuer and fund assessments\nIssuer scores are stored against canonical issuer IDs. They consider published disclosures, operational controls and identified controversies within this synthetic scenario. Fund scores are separate analyst assessments of portfolio characteristics, exclusions, stewardship and evidence quality. A fund score is not the arithmetic average of its holding scores. Consequently, a high-scoring fund can still own a prohibited issuer when its strategy allows that activity.\n\nAn unapproved or unavailable score is null. It must not be converted to zero, estimated from another fund, or filled from an archived assessment. Every fund subject to a minimum-score mandate requires its own current approved assessment. At 30 September, Emerging Markets Opportunities has a pending fund assessment, and Aquila Biotech (ISS-055) has a missing issuer score. The separate Aquila Bio Technologies (ISS-059) is a different issuer.\n\n## 3. Restricted activities\nThe issuer register uses tobacco, weapons and fossil_fuels tags. For this training policy, a verified direct involvement in manufacturing or supplying these restricted products creates the relevant tag; the dataset does not implement revenue thresholds. Exposure includes the entire value of a position in a tagged issuer. Equity and debt both count. Tags can overlap, so activity exposures must not be added and described as a mutually exclusive asset allocation.\n\nOrient Leaf Products is classified as Consumer Staples but is tobacco-tagged. A sector-only tobacco rule would miss it. Redleaf Tobacco is both sector-labelled and activity-tagged. The classification_status field distinguishes verified records from missing evidence; an absent verified tag list is different from an unknown classification.\n\n## 4. Decisions and exceptions\nClient mandates determine permitted exposure and may be stricter than a fund strategy. Engagement does not cancel an exclusion. Unknown identity, missing classifications, stale evidence or unavailable required scores create REVIEW. A verified positive exposure against a zero-tolerance rule creates BREACH. The LLM may describe the evidence but cannot override a calculated result or infer that an investment is suitable.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-001'},
    {'document_id': 19, 'path': 'documents/DOC-POL-002.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 19, 'title': 'Sustainability Claims and Stewardship Policy', 'document_type': 'sustainability_policy', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-002'}, 'content': "# Sustainability Claims and Stewardship Policy\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-POL-002  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Objective\nWrenford aims to describe sustainability characteristics accurately and keep investment claims proportionate to available evidence. A company operating in renewable energy or healthcare is not automatically sustainable in every respect. Research must consider its operations, supply chain, governance and the quality of the evidence. This policy governs internal wording and stewardship records in the fictional training firm.\n\n## 2. Strategy commitments\nSustainable Leaders excludes tobacco-, weapons- and fossil_fuels-tagged issuers according to the approved issuer register. Global Corporate Bond excludes tobacco and weapons. The other four fund strategies do not promise blanket activity exclusions. A restriction in one strategy must not be represented as a firm-wide exclusion across all products. Client mandates can apply additional limits regardless of a product's permitted universe.\n\nThe Sustainable Leaders portfolio includes renewable generation, water services, recycling and selected technology investments. Its holdings support a theme-based research approach, but the dataset contains no quantified avoided-emissions measure, independently certified impact result or legal sustainability label. An assistant must state that those conclusions are unsupported if asked.\n\n## 3. Engagement and escalation\nAnalysts record the issue, evidence requested, owner and follow-up date for each engagement. Greenhaven Water is asked to provide leakage-reduction evidence; Suncrest Storage is asked for battery recycling controls. A request for information does not establish that the requested outcome has occurred. Engagement progress can inform research, while confirmed excluded activity still triggers the applicable exclusion.\n\nWhere issuer evidence deteriorates, the research team reviews the classification before communicating claims. Compliance determines whether a client restriction has been breached, and the portfolio manager proposes any response. The firm does not promise an automatic sale solely because a narrative mentions controversy. The approved mandate and breach procedure determine next steps.\n\n## 4. Client communication\nSummaries should separate the fund's objective, its verified holdings and its manager's expectations. Avoid describing internal ESG scores as universal measures of ethical quality. Do not say that all Wrenford products are fossil-free. Do not infer that Sustainable Leaders is suitable for a particular client from its name or score. Unavailable evidence should be described plainly, with a citation to the relevant document or register record.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-002'},
    {'document_id': 20, 'path': 'documents/DOC-POL-003.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 20, 'title': 'Fund Fees and Performance Presentation Methodology', 'document_type': 'fee_methodology', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-003'}, 'content': "# Fund Fees and Performance Presentation Methodology\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-POL-003  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Fee definitions\nEvery fee percentage in this dataset is an annual percentage of fund assets for the fictional GBP accumulation share class. The management fee pays for portfolio management. Other ongoing costs cover administration, custody and associated recurring services. Ongoing charges equal the management fee plus these other ongoing costs. None of the six funds charges a performance fee.\n\nEstimated transaction costs are shown separately and are not included in the ongoing-charge figure. They reflect costs associated with trading the underlying assets and may vary. Platform fees and adviser fees are outside the fund charge schedule and are not supplied by this dataset. The assistant must not invent them when calculating a client's all-in cost.\n\n## 2. Range comparison\nGlobal Quality charges 0.75% management plus 0.15% other ongoing costs, giving 0.90% ongoing charges. Sustainable Leaders totals 1.00%; Emerging Markets Opportunities 1.30%; UK Equity Income 0.75%; Technology Innovation 1.35%; and Global Corporate Bond 0.55%. Technology Innovation has the highest ongoing charge in this range, and Global Corporate Bond the lowest. This comparison does not imply either product is better or suitable.\n\n## 3. Illustration of cost\nFor a constant £100,000 balance, 0.90% annual ongoing charges produce an illustrative £900 annual amount. The separate 0.08% estimated transaction-cost figure would represent £80 on the same constant balance. The combined illustration is £980 before any unknown external fees. Actual costs depend on asset values, trading and holding period. Portfolio illustrations sum each fund weight multiplied by its annual charge rate, divided by 100.\n\n## 4. Returns and comparator treatment\nFactsheet returns are sterling total returns with income reinvested, net of ongoing charges. Transaction costs are reflected in the underlying fund value. Adviser and platform charges are excluded. Do not subtract ongoing charges again from an already net return. Benchmark returns are fictional comparator returns without a client-level platform or adviser charge.\n\nRelative performance is reported as fund return minus benchmark return in percentage points. Emerging Markets Opportunities at -2.4% versus 1.1% is therefore 3.5 percentage points behind, rather than a 3.5% loss relative to initial capital. Trailing 12-month returns cover the year ending 30 September 2026; quarter returns cover 1 July–30 September. Yield, quarter return and annual fee rates are different measures and must not be added as though they shared one period.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-003'},
    {'document_id': 21, 'path': 'documents/DOC-POL-004.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 21, 'title': 'Mandate Breach, Evidence Review and Data Freshness Procedure', 'document_type': 'compliance_breach_procedure', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-004'}, 'content': "# Mandate Breach, Evidence Review and Data Freshness Procedure\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-POL-004  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Screening outcomes\nDeterministic screening produces PASS, BREACH or REVIEW for each rule. PASS means the specific rule is satisfied by complete current evidence. BREACH means a verified measurement violates the signed threshold. REVIEW means a required conclusion cannot be established because of missing, stale or ambiguous evidence. Overall status is BREACH if any rule breaches; otherwise REVIEW if any rule needs review; otherwise PASS.\n\nThe status of a known breach cannot be lowered to REVIEW because another field is missing. An assistant cannot convert a result to PASS on the basis of a narrative justification. PASS covers the mandate restrictions tested and does not establish investment suitability or regulatory approval.\n\n## 2. Evidence and dates\nHoldings and portfolio allocations must reconcile to 100% and refer to the same snapshot date. The routine freshness limit is 90 calendar days, measured against the requested screening date. A source becomes stale only when its age exceeds 90 days; 90 days exactly is acceptable. A future-dated source is invalid for a past-date screen and needs review. A document may be fresh for a historical screen and stale for a current screen.\n\nThe 28 February Emerging Markets factsheet is archived and superseded. At 30 September it is 214 days old and fails the freshness limit. It must not supply the current missing ESG score. Normally only current versions are eligible for current-state Q&A. Historical questions can retrieve archived versions when the answer states the date and version explicitly.\n\n## 3. Operational escalation\nRecord the portfolio, mandate version, rule ID, source dates, calculated exposure and contribution detail when a breach is identified. Notify the assigned compliance function through the firm's authorised workflow. In this dataset, no notification is actually sent; reports provide review material for the future application. The portfolio manager should avoid increasing the affected exposure until compliance has reviewed the exception.\n\nCompliance verifies the data and mandate wording, records any immediate control and requests a remediation proposal. The client adviser explains confirmed findings using plain language. A trade, mandate amendment or client communication requires the applicable human authorisation; the assistant cannot perform or approve these decisions on its own.\n\n## 4. Closure and audit\nClose an exception only after the approved action has been completed and a fresh screen confirms the result. Retain the original breach, subsequent calculations and approval trail. Name matching must use resolved issuer IDs; ambiguous matches such as Aquila Biotech and Aquila Bio Technologies require review. The current dataset deliberately contains an ESG evidence exception for Ashcombe and confirmed concentration breaches for Hartwell.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-004'},
    {'document_id': 22, 'path': 'documents/DOC-POL-005.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 22, 'title': 'Suitability Review Training Guidance — Fictional Internal Standard', 'document_type': 'suitability_guidance', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': '', 'portfolio_id': '', 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-005'}, 'content': "# Suitability Review Training Guidance — Fictional Internal Standard\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-POL-005  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## 1. Status and purpose\nThis is a fictional internal training standard. It is not legislation, a quotation of a regulator's handbook or a statement of current legal requirements. Its purpose is to provide realistic source material for a learning application that explains the boundary between mandate checks and a human suitability review. No real regulatory approval is represented by this document.\n\n## 2. Information considered by the adviser\nWithin the training workflow, the adviser records investment objectives, expected holding period, liquidity needs, knowledge and experience, financial circumstances, tolerance for volatility and capacity for loss. The adviser checks whether that information is complete and current before forming an investment recommendation. A fund's risk band or ESG score cannot substitute for understanding these client circumstances.\n\nThe synthetic portfolios contain client names, allocations, values and mandates but do not contain a full suitability assessment. A signed allocation restriction tells the system which limits to check; it does not prove that the selected investments meet all of a client's needs. No return forecast, tax recommendation or suitability conclusion can be justified from this limited pack.\n\n## 3. How screening supports review\nThe tool calculates indirect exposure and compares it with approved rules. It can establish that a tobacco exclusion or concentration cap has been breached. That result should be escalated even if the investment performed well. A PASS result means the tested rules were satisfied using available evidence; it does not mean the client should buy, retain or sell the investment.\n\nAn adviser can use grounded summaries to explain fees, strategy and reported performance. Quantitative facts should cite a current factsheet, and performance explanations should identify manager commentary as the manager's account rather than a verified attribution analysis. If the documents do not provide a numerical breakdown, the assistant should say so.\n\n## 4. Assistant boundary and refusal\nThe assistant should decline requests to make a final personalised suitability decision, guarantee returns, fabricate missing information or conceal a calculated breach. It may instead describe the available facts and identify the review required. Questions about real laws, tax treatment or current regulatory requirements require verified external sources and qualified review; this fictional guidance must not be presented as authoritative law.\n\nFor grounded knowledge answers, insufficient relevant evidence should produce an explicit refusal. In the future application, if no retrieved document clears the configured relevance floor, that refusal must occur before an LLM call. The numerical relevance threshold requires empirical evaluation and is intentionally not prescribed by this synthetic policy.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-POL-005'},
    {'document_id': 23, 'path': 'documents/DOC-PRT-001.md', 'metadata': {'document_id': 23, 'title': 'Haven Cancer Support Growth Portfolio — Allocation and Screening Report', 'document_type': 'portfolio_report', 'firm': 'Wrenford Asset Management', 'as_of_date': '2026-09-30', 'published_date': '2026-09-30', 'version': '1.0', 'status': 'current', 'fund_id': '', 'mandate_id': 1, 'portfolio_id': 1, 'supersedes_document_id': '', 'synthetic': True, 'source_document_key': 'DOC-PRT-001'}, 'content': "# Haven Cancer Support Growth Portfolio — Allocation and Screening Report\n\n> SYNTHETIC — fictional educational dataset. All organisations, people, securities, benchmarks, figures and events are invented.\n\n**Document ID:** DOC-PRT-001  \n**Owner:** Wrenford Asset Management  \n**As at:** 2026-09-30  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Status:** current\n\n## Portfolio purpose and scope\n\nHaven Cancer Support Growth Portfolio is a fictional client allocation maintained for mandate-screening research. The current valuation is £2,000,000. Its approved mandate is MND-001, documented in DOC-MND-001. This report uses the 30 September 2026 snapshot and complete look-through holdings. It does not assess the client's wider circumstances.\n\n## Fund allocation\n\n| Fund ID | Fund | Portfolio weight | Indicative value GBP |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| FND-001 | Wrenford Global Quality Equity Fund | 50.00% | 1,000,000 |\n| FND-002 | Wrenford Sustainable Leaders Fund | 30.00% | 600,000 |\n| FND-006 | Wrenford Global Corporate Bond Fund | 20.00% | 400,000 |\n\n**Total allocation: 100.00%.**\n\n## Recorded screening outcome\n\nOverall status: **BREACH**. The screening result is derived from current structured data; it is not an LLM judgement. The individual rules are recorded below.\n\n| Rule | Target | Actual | Condition | Outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| RUL-01-01 | tobacco | 2.000 | = 0.0 | BREACH |\n| RUL-01-02 | weapons | 1.750 | = 0.0 | BREACH |\n| RUL-01-03 | ISS-001 | 5.350 | <= 5.0 | BREACH |\n| RUL-01-04 | Technology | 18.650 | <= 25.0 | PASS |\n| RUL-01-05 | FUND | 72.000 | >= 70.0 | PASS |\n| RUL-01-06 | FUND | 5.000 | <= 5.0 | PASS |\n\nThe following verified breaches require compliance review: RUL-01-01, RUL-01-02, RUL-01-03. A favourable fund commentary or previous performance does not waive these restrictions. The manager should record the finding and prepare a remediation proposal for authorised review.\n\n## Look-through issuer example\n\n| Fund | Portfolio weight | LumenArc weight in fund | Portfolio contribution |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| FND-001 | 50.00% | 8.00% | 4.000% |\n| FND-002 | 30.00% | 2.50% | 0.750% |\n| FND-006 | 20.00% | 3.00% | 0.600% |\n\nTotal LumenArc Systems (ISS-001) exposure is **5.350%**. The calculation includes bond and equity positions. Verified aliases share this same canonical identity.\n\n## Fee illustration\n\nWeighted annual ongoing charges are 0.860%, or approximately £17,200.00 on a constant valuation. Separate estimated transaction costs are 0.072%. Adviser and platform fees are unavailable. These are simple illustrations rather than projected invoices.\n\n## Follow-up\n\nUse DOC-POL-004 for breach and evidence handling, the associated mandate for the legal wording within this fictional scenario, and the fund factsheets for holdings. Recalculate the report if allocations, holdings, classifications or rules change. These values are expected results for the supplied seed snapshot; the future application must calculate them from its live records rather than return this report as a hardcoded tool response.\n", 'source_document_key': 'DOC-PRT-001'},
